{
  "segment_function": "argument",
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Smith and Marx both shared a modern contempt for unproductive labor, viewing it as parasitical or a perversion because it does not enrich the world.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "Moreover, both Smith and Marx were in agreement with modern public opinion when they despised unproductive labor as parasitical, actually a kind of perversion of labor, as though nothing were worthy of this name which did not enrich the world.",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "qualifications": [],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "claim": "Marx's introduction of the term 'labor power' (Arbeitskraft) was the most original and revolutionary element of his system, as it explains labor's productivity through the production of a surplus beyond its own reproduction.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "It is because not labor itself but the surplus of human “labor power ” ( Arbeits kraft) explains labor’s productivity that Marx’s introduction of this term, as Engels rightly remarked, constituted the most original and revolutionary element of his whole system.",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "qualifications": [],
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
  ],
  "definitions": [
    {
      "term": "productivity of labor power",
      "definition": "A productivity that produces objects only incidentally and is primarily concerned with the means of its own reproduction, capable of producing a surplus beyond what is necessary for its own reproduction."
    }
  ],
  "distinctions": [
    {
      "item_a": "productive labor",
      "item_b": "unproductive labor",
      "distinction": "Unproductive labor was historically despised as parasitical and leaving nothing behind, whereas the modern social viewpoint treats all laboring as productive from the perspective of the life process of mankind."
    },
    {
      "item_a": "productivity of work",
      "item_b": "productivity of labor power",
      "distinction": "The productivity of work adds new objects to the human artifice, whereas the productivity of labor power produces objects only incidentally and is primarily concerned with the means of its own reproduction."
    }
  ],
  "references_to_other_sections": [],
  "unresolved_context": [],
  "must_not_be_lost": [
    "The modern age and Marx tended to look upon all labor as work and to speak of animal laborans in terms fitting for homo faber.",
    "Within a completely socialized mankind guided by Marx's ideal, the distinction between labor and work would completely disappear as all things become functions of the life process."
  ]
}

